ef 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1045 "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106007 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e36ee21c-371b-5637-8539-624dc138ba57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3535bc96-e0a2-5dcb-9a5f-4a6bf759302b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106055 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d136f69-0617-5d12-ad3b-b952611b8b09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106377 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1061 17192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b66da6f-e7b3-547c-b47a-3bfe17bbc205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1061 17193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "414f018d-78f3-5c78-86b8-6363d86d3a9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1061 17194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00d5123d-64a7-5878-9b67-ac7b7a2bf0a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1061 17195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc00947a-2ca5-59a7-a7a8-349b27d59bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106639 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106639 #edb/ref 1061 17206 #edb/