#edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1045 "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3b658871-23f8-5b8d-92ee-5294fec9bba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "947396a7-1dd0-5962-800b-c42f03f930a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115744 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a9a3feb-6c16-50e8-885d-ee5cd242ab95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115753 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nI. It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n\nII. It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "995f3b18-ad68-56fb-a94d-9e7f0a50f6c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115882 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116024 #edb/ref 1061 11842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "646dc860-2777-5e44-aa90-555d9b22ef5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116025 #edb/ref 1061 11843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3690006-d413-5d43-b44b-7db5a3b33035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115179 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
